<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Endless research (system: research, tracker item research.endless). What a civilization that has finished
    all 245 authored projects keeps its researchers on. Each track continues a tag the authored tree already
    uses, so the tail grows out of the tree rather than beside it.

    A track is a vocabulary, not a list. The first version of this file gave each track three authored titles
    and let EndlessResearch append Roman numerals once they ran out, which is exactly as endless as counting
    is. What a track carries now is the three word-lists a generated name is composed from - substrates (what
    this domain studies), foundationForms (what a foundational result in it is called) and appliedForms (what
    an applied one is called) - and the fourth word comes from the age (EndlessAgeDefs/EndlessAges.xml). The
    supply of names is the product of those lists across an unbounded register of ages, not the length of any
    one of them.

    Two rules the content has to keep, both asserted by the tests rather than by ConfigErrors, because both
    are properties of the shipped SET rather than of any one Def:

      - substrates are disjoint between tracks. This is what keeps a matter project from borrowing a mind
        noun, and it is also half the proof that no two generated labels can ever be the same.
      - foundationForms and appliedForms are disjoint within a track, so a label's last word says whether the
        project is the spine of its age or optional work hanging off it.

    Six tracks, not fourteen and not three. The authored tree's fourteen tracks are where the history of ideas
    is actually told; past its end the distinctions that matter are coarser. Six is enough that an age has
    real breadth to choose within, and few enough that an age is legible as one thing.

    baseCost and costExponent are this port's own judgement - RimWorld has no endless tech to source them
    from. What pins them is a relationship, not a literal: a generated project starts at or above the most
    expensive authored project (9000), and the cost curve is polynomial rather than geometric so the tail
    keeps getting harder without becoming a wall. The tests assert the trend.
  -->

